ish  translation but she knew the math she in  fact went beyond what Newton had done  and analyzed his equations and she  figured out that you could do an  experiment she thinks she's the one who  figured out that you know both momentum  and energy are conserved under the right  circumstances again I don't know the  history very very well but what it came  down to was dropping objects onto a  surface that could deform and sort of  absorb the energy so like a clay surface  or something like that rather than a  cement surface and then you could show  that the amount of deformation depended  on 1/2 MV squared on the energy that was  contained there in the falling object so  she's the one who showed that both  momentum and energy are conserved  they're not the only ones there are  other things that are conserved electric  charge is conserved right we are made of  electrons protons and neutrons neutrons  have zero electric charge protons are +1  electrons or -1 so you will never see  for example a proton turning into an  electron that would violate the  conservation of charge of course would  also violate conservation of energy also  but what you can see is a neutron  turning into an electron and a proton  and an anti-neutrino so neutrons charge  zero protons charge +1 electrons charge  -1 neutrinos or anti neutrinos charge 0  so one Neutron can turn into a proton  and electron and an anti-neutrino  conserving both energy and charge at the  same time so this is a big deal ok this  idea of conservation laws there's no  conservation laws in Aristotelian  physics in Aristotle you know if you  come across that box sitting on the  ground and you say well what conserved  quantities are there you don't know  there there aren't any it may have been  moving right it may have had energy or  momentum before but it came to arrest  and we don't know what its past was just  from its current perspective whereas if  you believe in these conservation laws  then you shift your perspective