ilities let  me show you this is actually an award  that I won this is a prize given by the  graduate students at the University of  Chicago is a teaching award and it's a  spherical cow award get it see the  spherical cow the point is the spherical  cow philosophy takes us really far this  is a special thing about physics that  wouldn't be true typically in biology or  farming or psychology or political  science or economics or anything like  that  the idea that we can ignore hugely  important features of the world get an  answer in a dramatically simplified  context and then add back in the rest of  the world is sort of a small correction  right the true nature of motion is to  move and conserve momentum and then you  add in air resistance and friction and  dissipation and you can get the right  answer that is a technique that works in  a wide variety of physics circumstances  that just often isn't available when  objects in the world are really talking  to each other varies  strongly now there's areas of physics  nonlinear dynamics and other areas where  objects do talk to each other very  strongly and you could not ignore the  interactions but for much of physics the  spherical cal approximation works pretty  well not only did the invention of  conservation of momentum mark a shift  from a vocabulary of nature's and  purposes to a vocabulary of patterns and  equations it also marked the  introduction of this spherical cow  philosophy that works so well it's why  if you sort of turn it on its head a  little bit  why does physics seem hard because  physics is easy that's the answer  because in physics we can make these  hilariously inaccurate idealizations and  approximations and still learn something  in a way that in other areas of science  we can't this has allowed us to think  about individual electrons and atoms and  things like that and make enormous  progress on the basis of those thoughts  that's why physics is hard because  physics is so easy that we've l